  urldate = {2024-05-22},
  abstract = {THE TIMES, MONDAY, MAKCfl 20, 1865. ' RK - rKAUGUnATION OP PRESIDENT - . V I I vmr vr A ' . , - . - (mo - our owx cDRRKsroxnEjrr.V vrieiiti.nJiv. . ' . Four year, ago Abraham Lincoln, elected by a ,.,jor.y of the States, but by a minority of the American jW,le, took the oath of office adminis - thet,,vd to him bv the Chief JnatU nf ih Sm uourt, an ardent pro - alavrry politician and friend of the Southern cause. Washington wm more than half rebellious, and it was. at the risk of his life that the now President whoso personal character and ability wore then little known or appreciated ventured to show himsclfin public. Very different were the rircumstanees of hU io - installstion yesterday; Though fully one - third of the States took Ho part u itu n - viecuou ana considered turn a foreigner, and the bitter enemy of 'their' libortr and inde pendence, the other two - thinls re - appointed him to office by a majority so triumphant as to prove that in spite of many errors, shortcoming, and lailuroa ho. was their deliberate . choice for tho lushest honour they could confer, and tho only man who; in their judgment, was fit to carry to it cloaa the fearful war of which his .first election hid been the signal. . On this second' occasion, aahinst,m, - which was crammed to ovcrflow iiv; with tisitbrs from all parU of tho country, instead of receiving Mr. Lincoln witli distrust, or even' with .'scant courtOsy, was Ardent in tho exproasioii of its joy. If he h.vl an enemy in - the capital nono Mich dared to show, hini - .u'Jf. Ho drove unprotccta.1 through tho streets mn ojien carria', rKceivinj at every plica, tho vociferous plaudju of the multitude, and when at last he arrived in tho Senate Chamber and walked tothe platform, where in the presence of the people the oath was to bo administered, he took it from tho lips of a Chief Jmtico of his own appointment, a inanjwhoso opinions on the jrreat questions' which nsiUta - the country and underlie its mighty struggle for existence are identical with his own. Tho night prerious to the inauguration was cold and rainy. Tho Senate and House of Representa tives, both of which sat till daylight of the 4th to drMiatch tho Vast arrears of business which tbor sutTored to accumulate, were' startled at short intervals by sharp gusts of wind and hail 'which battered on the skylights, and which on one occasion, in the. dim grey of the early dawn, suddenly burst with such ;fury as to suggest tho idea of an explo sion within tho building. Many of tho members rose, aarigh ted from their scats and rushed towards tho door. The deliberations were for a ' while .suspended in. tho general alarm, .until the Sjieaker, rising from, his .chair, begged tho representatives to resume their places, announcing that tho noiso " was only a storm." When day broke the rain was falling in tor rents will scarcely ceased Until 10 o'clock. Washington, never free from horrible mud in wet weather and from still more horrible dust in tho dry, was more than ankle - deep in the middle of the streets and ankle - deep at least in the fo'otpatlis. Kvery ono, predicted that the ceremonial of tho day would, take place under cover, in tho. Senate Cham. bcr, and that tho unprivileged - multitude would lose their share of the. spectacle. At 11 o'clock, however, the; storm began to moderate, and a streak of light in tho far horizon gave promise of fair weather. The processions ' speedily began to form, including regi - meiitv - of soldiers in their shabby blue uniforms horse, fool, and artillery white men as well as black deputations of firemen from Baltimore, Phil adelphia, and other cities, Masonic Lodges, Friendly Societies, all with bands of music and banners, amid which the" "green flag of Erin" - was next in con - spiouousness to the all - pervading stars and stripes. From the White House, down the wide and pretentious but .mean - looking thoroughfare called Penn sylvania - avenue, the Broadway and High - street of Washington, which connects tho Presidential man sion with tho Capitol, all was life and - bustle; In spite of tho miro and the weather: the streets, swarmed with people. The women trailed their long garments through the slush," as if silks were of no value, and the men trampled on heed lessly - in long boots worn over their trousers, through mud. and filth of a consistency and depth which no European, who did not know .the contrary from his American experience could believo to be compatible with the government of a civilized city. It was remarked by everybody, - strangers .is well as natives, that there never had been seen such crowds of negroes in the streets of the capital. At least one - half of ' the . multitude wero coloured people, pouring in from far and near to " assist " in the ceremonial of a day which to them and to "many wiser people seemed tho triumph of their race over a fast fading social prejudice and political injustice. The negresses, "dressed all in "their best," flaunted in red, blue, yellow, and every variety of brilliant and gaudy colour, and looked as buoyantly happy as servant girls usually do when out for a holyday. The negroes held their heads high, as if they thoroughly understood that, under, the beneficent sway of Abraham Lincoln, " man was a man for a that ;'' if even, ho were not something better than a manif his skin happened to bo of the Ethiopian and not of the Caucasian colour, Oh reaching the Senate Chamber, about half - past 11, I' found tho spacious galleries crowded with; ladies; as brilliant \&amp;3 a parterre of flowers at midsummer. Tho Senate was devoting its attention daring the last few minutes of the Session, which was legally to expire at noon, to the all - engrossing question of the negro. - The negro was lord of the hourl and. .the - place, and tho Senate was but his minuter. Tho motion before the House was the parsing of a bill to punish by fine of 500, and by imprisonment for any period not shorter than three montlis,or longer than three years, any driver or conductor of any railroad cur, or omnibus, brstoamship, or public conveyance of any kind whatever, who should eject therefrom or refuse to admit therein any person on account of bjs or her colour. To this proposal sonio senator whether, in earnest or derision it was impossible to ascertain, or even to hear, on account of tho incessant buzz and chatter kept up by the ladies in tho galleries which tho Senate was cither ..too IndilTereut or too gallant to notice moved an - ad - . ditiori, to - the effect that tho same penalties should apply' to any hotel or inn keeper who should refuse accommodation in his house to any negro or nogress, or mulatto, male, or female, who applied for admission like .other travellers. Tho Senato - Jiad not decided this question, nor had time to ask howany penal enactment .could reach a hotelkeepcr who, not likingnegro company, shouldsimply inform anynegro or negrcss wishing to pay for his hospitality that his house was full, when the arrival of some distinguished persons drew all eyes to tho gangway and put an end to all further discussion. Whether the Bill had pa - ied with or without the amendment, or whether it had dropped through altogether, no one. could tell. Tho new comers were Mr. Hannibal namlm, Vice - President of tho United States, and ex officio President of tho Senate, and Mr. Andrew Johnson, of Tennessee, tho Vice - President elect. Tho acting President, Mr. Andrew Foote, cordially. shook hands with both, gentlemen and vacated tho clair, which ' Mr. Hamlin assumed, and beckoned Mn Johnson to a seat ,bcsido him. There entered immediately afterwards Mr. Seward, Mr. Stanton, Mr. W elles, and other members of the Cabinet, who took their places on chairs reserved for them to the right of the presi ding officer. Scarcely had they seated themselves. when tho justices of tho Supreme Court, in their black sdk gowns, precoded by Chief Juttico Chase, looking every inch liko a judge, entered and took their places on the left, and wero closely followed by the Foreign Ministers, conspicuous among whom wero iU doIlaislofT, the Danish, and Baron btocckl, the Russian Ambassador, aud a Whole bevy of smaller magnate from other countries. Their brilliant uniforms were conspicuous amid tho sombre co'stnmcs of the senators and Ministers, and lit up tho place with a radiance agreeable enough to my r.uropean oyea, but which an irreverent lew Englander, who stood next to me, assured me con fidentially was " gimcrack" and " llunkeyism," and a kind of thing that did not suit a free country. It was retuarkod that England and Franco wero re presented on this occasion by altacki only, and that Mexico was not represented at all. Mr. Hamlin rose as soon as tho littlo fluttc caused by tficsa arrivals had subsided, and, in a short, unpretending, and effective speech, took his otticial farewell of tho body over whoso delibora Hons ho had presided for tho last four years, and concluded by introducing his. successor, tho Hon. Andrew - Johnson, of Tennessee, Vice - President Elect. AH eyes were turned to Mr. Johnson as he started, rather than rose, from his chair, and, with wild gesticulations and shrieks, strangely and weirdly intermingled with audible stage whispers, began to address tho brilliant auditory' around and above him. such oratory it. was never my fortune to hear before, and I hope never to hear again. Mr, Johnson was once a journeyman tailor, and rose by industry and the possession of something like politi cal genius to his present high position. In no froo country and least of all in America would any nglit - mindoa man seek to cast a slur upon him on aocount'of tho humbleness of his origin. No one thinks tho worse of Mr. Lincoln, - because in early life he was a rail - splitter and a boatman ; but if, as President of tho United States, ho behaved liko a rail - splitter, spoke like one, drank like ono, thought like one, and could not import into the higher sphero of his new lifo anything but tho vulgar manners and gross habits of tho old, it would be impossible for any one to forget his origin, or not - to lament that circumstances had lifted him out of a sphere in which it would ha vo been better if ho h.vl remained. It is not, therefore, becanso ho wasonco a journeyman tiilor that every witness of tho exhibition which Mr. Johnson mado of himself on tho day that Ought to have been tho proudest of his lifo feels humiliated at tho idea that such a person should have.arison from - tho dregs of isociety, to disgrace not alono tho dignity of his official position, but even the honest working classes from whom ho sprang. ' His behaviour was that of an illiterate,. vulgar,' and drunken rowdy, and, could it havo been displayed before any other legislative assembly in tho world, would have lod to his arrest by the serjeant - at - arms, if s not to his ignominious expulsion by tho deliberate votq of his insulted colleagues. Ho had not uttered - two sentences, when, everybody saw that somethin: was wrong. "Ho is drunk," said one. "Ho is " crazy," said another. - " This is disgraceful," said a third. - Mr. Seward and thoMinisterslooked on the ground, or moved uneasily in their scats. - The Judges of tha Supreme Court manifested by their faces their pain and their surprise. Mr. Johnson was so proud of the dignity into which fats had thrust him that he boasted of it in the languago of a clown and with tho manners of a co3termonger. "I am " a - going for to tell you hero to - day yes, lam " a - going for to tell - yOu all that I am a plebeian. " I glory in it, I am a plebeian. The people " yes, the people of' tho United States, tho great " people have made, me what I am ; and I am " a - going for to tell you hero to - day. yes, to - day, " in this' place that tho peoplo are everything. owe all to them. If ltbonot too presumptuous", " I will tell tho foreign Ministers a - sittiii' there " that I am ono of the people. I will say to sena - " tors and