s who had been elected within tho previous three months, among - whpin wa3.Mr.Fe3senden, tho late Secretary of th6 Treasury. It was thori' announced to tho President lof to toco by the Marshal that the weather was clear, that tho sun. was shining brilliantly, and that all was ready for tho ceremonial of tho day on tho platform erected on tho steps of tho Capitol.. Mr. Lincoln rose ; tho Ministers, tho Judges of the Supremo Court, and, a large body of tho senators followed. . Therb was a general rush and scrarublo in all parts of - tlio House, like the emptying of a theatre, when the pertormances aro over, but far more, eager, inasmuch a3 every ono strove who should bo first to ' reach a good dace to witness a new performance more interesting than, tho last. Every ono was left to shift for himself, and tho members of the Corps Diplomatique, with all their lino feathers and uniforms, sashes and ribands, stars and. crosses, fared no be'tter than the common crowd, and wero lelt to light their way into or out of the' mass, as it best pleased tiem. Most of them were", so displeased at the want of arrangement, or tho want of courtesy, whichever - it might havo been, that they mado no attempt to J follow tho President, and consequently took no part in the great celebration of tho day. Making my way out of tho building as fast as possible to minglo with tho crowd outside, I arrived just in time to hear Mr. Lincoln deliver the last words of his singular but pathetic, address. The document was' remarkably short, and did not ! occupy ten minutes in the reading. This done, a salute of 100 guus was fired in honour of the event, and Mr. Lincoln drove home again up Penn - procoasiott which accompanied him to tha CapitoL bowing graciously and not ungracefully to his lieges on every side, and taking their homago as naturally and unostentatiously as if ho were truly a king, and had been born in tho purple. Labouring through tha mire of the avenue half an hour afterwards, I met a distinguished citizen of Washington, and'a loading politician, who asked me if I had hoard 'the Vice - President's speech T J replied that I hail, and asked to what causa he at tributed, the humiliating display: " He was drunk ; ' ' very drunk," ho said. 11 Bu tit was not his fault. His "liquor was drugged by some Southern rebel, in "order to throw disgrace, upon the Federal Go - " vemment." " But," said I, " if such was the in tention, why did not the Southern rebcjjlrug the "President's drink rather than the Vice - Presi - ' dent's t" So ho would have done, I have no " doubt, only ho could not get at old Abe, who is,be - " sides, a sober man, and does not take drinks with " strangers." Meeting siorfly afterwards with a Democratic - senator, a moderate opponent of the Government, and no admirer of Mr. Lincoln or. any roemlcr of the Administration, he asked me " if "Iliad heard Andrew Johnson t" ".The country " is disgraced, he said, " and I pray God for the " health and long life of Abraham Lincoln. I " never prayed for him before, nor knew how " valuable his life was to tho country. Shonld " ho die within tho next four years, which "calamity may neaven in its mercy avert, we "should have - Andrew Johnson for President, and " sink to a lower depth of degradation than was " ever reached by any nation sinco tho Roman Em - " peror made his horso a consul." Tho editor of ono of the Washington journals hero stopped and took part in our conversation. " Do not," ho said, "describo this scene, to tho people, of England ( without telling them, at the same time, that the press of tho United States,' without distinction of party, were' ' down' upon Mr. Johnson for his "scandalous misconduct. Mark my words," he continued, " Mr. Johnson will never recover from " tho disgraco ho has brought upon himself. He is ruined as a - public man for ever." Iu accord ance with tho editor's suggestion, I record the fact that, as far as I havo yet seen, the Democratic or Opposition press, and the 